#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# n_dsolve

#% text
# `n_dsolve(eqs, y, [t, t0, t1])` solves the ordinary differential equations `eqs` with their initial conditions numerically for `t` from `t0` to `t1`, and gives `[y -> f]` with `f` an interpolating function.

#% text
# `n_dsolve(eqs, [y, z, ...], [t, t0, t1])` solves a system for several unknown functions.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Derivatives are written with primes: `y'(t)`, `y''(t)`. Initial conditions give the value of each unknown, and of its derivatives up to one less than the order, at `t0`.

#% item
# The result is a rule list; `y(t) /. sol` evaluates the solution at a point, and a plot of it draws the curve.

#% item
# Options:

#% subitem
# `method: auto | rk45 | stiff | taylor`: the integration method (`auto` changes to the stiff method when the problem needs it).

#% subitem
# `accuracy_goal`, `precision_goal`, `max_steps`, `max_step_size`: the error goals and the limits of the steps.

#% subitem
# `events: [g == 0]` stops the solution where `g` becomes zero (with `event_action: record`, it records the points and goes on).

#% subitem
# `digits: d` solves with Taylor series at `d` decimal digits; values of the solution are then balls.

#% item
# When the step size shrinks to nothing (the solution blows up), the solution covers the part computed and a message says where it stopped.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# Exponential decay, evaluated at t = 1:

#% cell
s = n_dsolve([y'(t) == -y(t), y(0) == 1], y, [t, 0, 2])

#% cell
y(1) /. s
#% output
#= 0.3678794409408397

#% text
# The solution is an interpolating function over the interval:

#% cell
s
#% output
#= [y -> interpolating_function("y", [0.0, 2.0], 21, "7d83dd8f58ce332a")]

#% text
# The logistic equation, and a plot of its solution:

#% cell
logistic = n_dsolve([y'(t) == y(t)*(1 - y(t)), y(0) == 0.1], y, [t, 0, 10])

#% cell
plot(y(t) /. logistic, [t, 0, 10])
#% output
#= image("n_dsolve.files/n_dsolve-5.png")

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A second-order equation, compared with its exact solution sin(t):

#% cell
s2 = n_dsolve([y''(t) + y(t) == 0, y(0) == 0, y'(0) == 1], y, [t, 0, 6])

#% cell
(y(2) /. s2) - sin(2)
#% output
#= -5.187932750061464e-9

#% text
# A system of two first-order equations, an oscillator, after half a period:

#% cell
osc = n_dsolve([x'(t) == v(t), v'(t) == -x(t), x(0) == 1, v(0) == 0], [x, v], [t, 0, 10])

#% cell
x(pi) /. osc
#% output
#= -0.9999999909648007

#% text
# The solution at several points at once:

#% cell
[y(0), y(5), y(10)] /. logistic
#% output
#= [0.1, 0.9428256207705609, 0.9995915655941424]

#% subsection
# Options

#% text
# A stiff equation, with the stiff method:

#% cell
stiff = n_dsolve([y'(t) == -1000*(y(t) - cos(t)), y(0) == 0], y, [t, 0, 1], method: stiff)

#% cell
y(1) /. stiff
#% output
#= 0.5411432357946486

#% text
# High precision with Taylor series:

#% cell
s3 = n_dsolve([y'(t) == y(t), y(0) == 1], y, [t, 0, 1], digits: 30)

#% cell
y(1) /. s3
#% output
#= 2.718281828459045235360287471353

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# A ball dropped from 10 m: the event stops the solution when it reaches the ground, and records the time.

#% cell
fall = n_dsolve([h'(t) == v(t), v'(t) == -9.81, h(0) == 10, v(0) == 0], [h, v], [t, 0, 5], events: [h(t) == 0])

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# The solution of y' = y^2 with y(0) = 1 is 1/(1 - t), which blows up at t = 1; the solution stops there:

#% cell
n_dsolve([y'(t) == y(t)^2, y(0) == 1], y, [t, 0, 2])
#% output
#| n_dsolve: the step size vanished (a singularity?) at t = 1.0000000010762093; the solution covers the part computed
#= [y -> interpolating_function("y", [0.0, 1.0000000010762093], 501, "7e5099b7fca175ab")]

#% text
# Without initial conditions there is no single solution to compute:

#% cell
n_dsolve([y'(t) == -y(t)], y, [t, 0, 1])
#% output
#! failure(argument, n_dsolve, "no initial conditions")

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `n_integrate`, `plot`, `find_root`, `d`
